---
/**
 * /stronger/vault/letters — line 03. The long-form room.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * WHY THIS ROOM EXISTS
 *
 * Everything else in the wing is short, and the shortness is correct for what it
 * is: the song takes a note because it is written at 7am one-handed, a photo
 * caption is one line because it sits on a panel in a 3D room. Added up, though,
 * that is a wing with nowhere to say anything at length — and this was built to
 * carry a year of long distance, which is not made of one-liners.
 *
 * So this page is prose and nothing else. No embeds, no reactions, no 3D, no
 * counters competing with the words. The measure, the leading and the ink are the
 * feature; see the header of public/css/us-letters.css for why none of them are
 * shared with the rest of the wing.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * THE SESSION IS RE-VERIFIED HERE, AND THAT IS NOT THEATRE
 *
 * src/middleware.ts has already checked a signed, unexpired token for anything
 * under /stronger/vault, or this render never happened. The check below is what
 * makes a routing mistake up there degrade to "she gets sent to the gate" instead
 * of "the internet gets my letters". Copied verbatim from the vault hub and from
 * today.astro so there is one pattern to audit rather than three.
 *
 * `session` specifically, NOT `session ?? admin`. My admin token cannot open this
 * page. Same deliberate inconvenience today.astro documents: the vault is hers,
 * that is one rule, and one rule is worth more than the convenience of previewing
 * her page without answering the questions.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * IT READS THE STORE DIRECTLY, NOT ITS OWN API
 *
 * This page is server-rendered, so calling /api/us/letter from here would mean the
 * server making an HTTP request to itself, forwarding her cookie, and paying a
 * round trip to reach a module it can just import. The endpoint exists for the
 * client; the page uses the store.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * TWO VIEWS, ONE FILE
 *
 *   no `?read`      THE SHELF. Every letter, grouped: waiting / again / sealed.
 *   `?read=<id>`    ONE LETTER, full width of its measure, and her reply under it.
 *
 * One file rather than two because the second view is a query parameter on the
 * first, which means the URL she gets back to is a normal link, the back button
 * behaves, and there is exactly one place that re-checks the session and one place
 * that owns the copy.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * THE SEAL IS ENFORCED ON THE SERVER
 *
 * A sealed letter's text is never in the response. visibleLetter() strips the body,
 * the signoff AND the title before this page ever holds the object, and `?read=` on
 * a sealed id is refused up here rather than hidden down there. `display: none` on
 * something I promised not to show her until October is a promise broken by View
 * Source.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * IT WORKS WITH JAVASCRIPT OFF
 *
 * Her reply is a real <form method="post" action="/api/us/letter"> that 303s back
 * here. The script at the bottom upgrades it to a fetch for ONE reason that is not
 * cosmetic: a native submit that fails has already navigated away and taken several
 * hundred words with it, and retyping a letter is the difference between answering
 * and not.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * THE READ RECEIPT IS WRITTEN ON RENDER, AND THAT IS A TRADE
 *
 * Opening a letter records that she opened it — a write on a GET. The alternative
 * (a fetch from the client, the way StudioRoom posts `seen`) is cleaner in theory
 * and does not work with JavaScript switched off, which this wing insists on
 * everywhere else. So: server-side, through the *Safe wrapper so a dead store costs
 * her the new/read badge and never the letter.
 *
 * THE COST, STATED: a browser that PREFETCHED a shelf link would mark that letter
 * read before she looked at it. Nothing here opts into prefetching — no
 * speculation rules, no rel=prefetch, and the middleware sends
 * `Cache-Control: private, no-store` on every response in the wing, which
 * suppresses it in the engines that do it speculatively. If that ever changes, this
 * is the line that breaks, and the fix is to move the receipt into the endpoint and
 * accept that it stops working without JavaScript.
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 */
import { HER_NAME, SESSION_SECRET } from '../../../lib/us/config';
import { readCookie, verify } from '../../../lib/us/session';
import { wingDate, wingDateLabel } from '../../../lib/us/kv';
import {
  REPLY_MAX,
  findLetter,
  getStatesSafe,
  isSealed,
  lettersTier,
  loadLetters,
  markReadSafe,
  shelf,
  summarize,
  usingPlaceholders,
  visibleLetter,
  type VisibleLetter,
} from '../../../lib/us/letters';

export const prerender = false;

const secret = SESSION_SECRET();
const session = secret ? verify(secret, 'session', readCookie(Astro.cookies, 'session', Astro.url)) : null;
if (!session) return Astro.redirect('/stronger', 302);

const her = HER_NAME();
const today = wingDate();

/* Read the state once, up front, and use it for everything. It is one pipelined
   round trip on Upstash and one GET on R2, and holding it in a variable is what
   stops the reading view and the shelf disagreeing about what she has read. */
const states = await getStatesSafe();

/* ---- which view -----------------------------------------------------------
   `read` is matched against the authored list by findLetter(), which SELECTS a
   record and never builds a key. So an id that is not a letter is simply not a
   letter: there is no path from this parameter to a store key, an R2 object or a
   file. `?read=../../.env` cannot select anything. */
const wanted = Astro.url.searchParams.get('read') ?? '';
const requested = wanted ? findLetter(wanted) : null;
const askedForMissing = Boolean(wanted) && !requested;

const view: VisibleLetter | null = requested ? visibleLetter(requested, today) : null;
/* Split into two variables rather than checked inline, so that every use below is
   unambiguous about whether it is allowed to see a body. `reading` is only ever an
   OPEN letter; a sealed request falls into `sealedRequest` and gets a card. */
const reading = view && !view.sealed ? view : null;
const sealedRequest = view && view.sealed ? view : null;

const herReply = reading ? (states[reading.id]?.reply ?? '') : '';
const herRepliedAt = reading ? (states[reading.id]?.repliedAt ?? 0) : 0;
/* Read BEFORE markReadSafe runs, so "you have had this open before" is a fact about
   her previous visits and not about this one. */
const readBefore = reading ? (states[reading.id]?.firstReadAt ?? 0) > 0 : false;

/* The receipt. Awaited rather than fired and forgotten: a serverless function that
   returns before its writes land does not finish them. It is the *Safe wrapper, so
   the worst a dead store can do is cost her the badge. */
if (reading) await markReadSafe(reading.id);

/* ---- the shelf ------------------------------------------------------------ */
const board = shelf(states, today);
const totals = summarize(states, today);
const nothingAtAll = board.waiting.length + board.again.length + board.sealed.length === 0;

/* ---- previous / next, among OPEN letters only -----------------------------
   Sealed ones are skipped rather than shown as an inaccessible step, because a
   "next" that refuses to open is a dead end with a label on it. In written order,
   which is loadLetters()' canonical order — a letter is part of a sequence. */
const openLetters = loadLetters().filter((l) => !isSealed(l, today));
const here = reading ? openLetters.findIndex((l) => l.id === reading.id) : -1;
/* Only the id is used, so these stay raw Letters rather than going through
   visibleLetter(): splitting a whole body into paragraphs to build a link is work
   for nothing, and the neighbour's TITLE deliberately does not appear on the button
   — "the one before" is enough, and naming it would put a second letter's title on
   the page she is trying to read. */
const older = here > 0 ? openLetters[here - 1] : null;
const newer = here >= 0 && here < openLetters.length - 1 ? openLetters[here + 1] : null;

/* ---- outcomes from the no-JavaScript reply path ---------------------------
   They arrive back here as a 303 with ?e=<code>, written in her language rather
   than in HTTP: she does not need to know what a 502 is, only that it was not her
   fault and it is worth another try. */
const errorCode = Astro.url.searchParams.get('e') ?? '';
const ERRORS: Record<string, string> = {
  store: "that didn't save — not your fault. Try it again? Your words are still in the box.",
  rate: 'okay, easy. Give it a minute and send it again.',
  unauthorized: 'your session ran out. Answer the questions again and you are back in.',
  unconfigured: 'something is unplugged on my end. Not your fault.',
  'bad-request': "the server couldn't read that. Try again?",
  'no-such-letter': "I can't find that one. It may have a different name now — everything is still here.",
  sealed: 'that one is still sealed. It opens on its own, I promise.',
  empty: 'there was nothing in the box.',
  'too-long': `that is longer than ${REPLY_MAX} characters and the box will not hold all of it. Trim it a little?`,
};
const errorText = errorCode ? (ERRORS[errorCode] ?? "that didn't go through. Try again?") : '';
const justSent = Astro.url.searchParams.get('sent') === '1';

/* ---- copy helpers --------------------------------------------------------- */

/**
 * "one", "two", ... "ten", then digits.
 *
 * Because "1 waiting for you" is a dashboard and "one waiting for you" is a
 * sentence, and this page is made of sentences. Past ten, digits read better than
 * words anyway.
 */
const SMALL = ['no', 'one', 'two', 'three', 'four', 'five', 'six', 'seven', 'eight', 'nine', 'ten'];
function countWord(n: number): string {
  return n >= 0 && n < SMALL.length ? SMALL[n] : String(n);
}

/**
 * The one line at the top of the shelf that answers "is there anything for me".
 *
 * Every branch is a real sentence and none of them prints a zero. A page that
 * greets her with "0 unread" is a page telling her this is empty and probably
 * broken — the same reasoning behind today.astro's conditional stat strip.
 */
function statusLine(): string {
  if (totals.unread === 1) return 'one waiting for you.';
  if (totals.unread > 1) return `${countWord(totals.unread)} waiting for you.`;
  if (totals.open > 0) return 'nothing new. Everything here keeps.';
  if (totals.sealed > 0) return 'nothing open yet.';
  return '';
}

/**
 * "about four minutes", or '' for anything short.
 *
 * Only shown past two minutes, and that threshold is the whole reason this is
 * tolerable: stamping a reading time on every letter would make them feel like
 * posts, but a letter she opens on a train deserves to say whether she has time for
 * it now. 220 words a minute is a normal adult rate for easy prose.
 */
function readingTime(paras: string[]): string {
  const words = paras.join(' ').split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean).length;
  const minutes = Math.round(words / 220);
  return minutes >= 2 ? `about ${countWord(minutes)} minutes` : '';
}

/** The title, or a description that reads as a description. */
function titleOf(letter: VisibleLetter): string {
  return letter.title || 'a letter I did not name';
}

/** e.g. `Sat 14 Feb 2026`. '' when the authored date was unusable. */
function dayLabel(date: string): string {
  return date ? wingDateLabel(date, { withYear: true }) : '';
}

/** Her reply, as paragraphs, so it is rendered exactly like a letter is. */
function replyParagraphs(text: string): string[] {
  return text
    .split(/\n{2,}/)
    .map((p) => p.replace(/\n/g, ' ').trim())
    .filter(Boolean);
}
---

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <meta name="color-scheme" content="dark">
    <!-- Belt and braces: the middleware also sends X-Robots-Tag, which covers the
         redirects and JSON responses that cannot carry a meta tag. -->
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow, noarchive">
    <meta name="referrer" content="no-referrer">
    <title>[us]</title>
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com">
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin>
    <!-- Space Grotesk and DM Mono only. THE READING FACE IS A SYSTEM SERIF AND
         THAT IS DELIBERATE — public/css/us-letters.css explains why the one page
         whose product is uninterrupted reading is the worst place to introduce a
         font swap. Do not add a webfont here without reading that comment. -->
    <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Space+Grotesk:wght@400;500;600;700&family=DM+Mono:wght@400;500&display=swap" rel="stylesheet">
    <!-- Absolute paths: this page is at /stronger/vault/letters, so a relative href
         would resolve to /stronger/vault/css/... and 404.

         us.css first — it owns every token the other two use. us-song.css second,
         and NOT because this page shows songs: it owns the wing's shared form and
         status primitives (.notice, .sr-only, .dj-*), and its own comment says to
         treat them that way. Redefining them here is the drift that comment warns
         about, and every rule in that file is class-scoped (there is not one bare
         element selector in it), so loading it has no side effects on this page. -->
    <!-- Installable-app metadata. `scope: /stronger/` in the manifest keeps the
         installed app inside the wing, so tapping the icon can never land her on
         the public portfolio, and `start_url: /stronger/vault` opens at the hub
         rather than the gate — she already holds a session cookie, so she skips
         the quiz on every launch.

         DELIBERATELY NO SERVICE WORKER. Every page here is behind a session and
         several are per-day; a worker that cached the wrong response would serve
         her a stale room, or worse, hand a cached authorised page to whoever
         opened the app next. Standalone display, the icon and the black status
         bar all work without one. The only thing given up is offline, which for
         a room whose whole content is fetched per visit is not a real loss. -->
    <link rel="manifest" href="/manifest.webmanifest">
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/assets/us/icons/apple-touch-icon.png">
    <meta name="apple-mobile-web-app-capable" content="yes">
    <meta name="apple-mobile-web-app-status-bar-style" content="black-translucent">
    <meta name="apple-mobile-web-app-title" content="[us]">
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#000000">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/us.css">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/us-song.css">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/us-letters.css">
    <link rel="icon" href="data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 32 32'%3E%3Crect width='32' height='32' fill='%23000'/%3E%3Ctext x='16' y='23' font-family='monospace' font-size='20' font-weight='700' text-anchor='middle' fill='%233e7bff'%3E%5B%5D%3C/text%3E%3C/svg%3E">
</head>
<body class="studio letters">
<main class="studio-wrap">

    {reading ? (
        /* ==============================================================
           VIEW TWO — ONE LETTER

           The letter's own title is the <h1>. The wordmark shrinks to a
           small link in the top bar, which is also the way back, because
           on a reading page the biggest words on screen should be the
           ones I wrote to her.
           ============================================================== */
        <>
            <div class="lt-topbar">
                <a class="lt-topbar-mark" href="/stronger/vault/letters">
                    <span class="us-mark-bracket" aria-hidden="true">[</span>letters<span
                          class="us-mark-bracket" aria-hidden="true">]</span>
                </a>
                <p class="lt-topbar-where">
                    line 03 &middot; {readBefore ? 'again' : 'for you'}
                </p>
            </div>

            {errorText && (
                <p class="notice" data-kind="bad" role="status">hm &mdash; {errorText}</p>
            )}
            {justSent && !errorText && (
                <p class="notice" data-kind="ok" role="status">
                    sent &mdash; it is on the shelf next to the letter. I will read it.
                </p>
            )}

            {/* An <article>, because a letter is a self-contained piece of
                content and that is precisely what the element is for. */}
            <article class="lt-column lt-sheet">
                {(dayLabel(reading.written) || readingTime(reading.paragraphs)) && (
                    <p class="lt-dateline">
                        {[dayLabel(reading.written), readingTime(reading.paragraphs)]
                            .filter(Boolean)
                            .join(' · ')}
                    </p>
                )}

                <h1 class={reading.title ? 'lt-title' : 'lt-title lt-title--untitled'}>
                    {titleOf(reading)}
                </h1>

                {/* Every paragraph is a real <p> holding ESCAPED text. Astro
                    escapes `{expression}` by default and there is no
                    `set:html` anywhere on this page, which is the whole
                    reason a letter is allowed to contain any character at
                    all. paragraphs() in letters.ts is the only thing that
                    interprets the body format. */}
                <div class="lt-prose">
                    {reading.paragraphs.map((para) => <p>{para}</p>)}
                </div>

                {reading.signoff && <p class="lt-signoff">{reading.signoff}</p>}
            </article>

            {/* ==========================================================
                HER HALF

                Same reading face as the letter, one step up in elevation,
                mirrored accent. today.astro makes the same call and states
                the reason: an exchange is not a receipt stapled to the
                bottom of a broadcast.
                ========================================================== */}
            <section class="lt-column lt-hers" aria-labelledby="hers-head">
                <p class="lt-hers-head" id="hers-head">
                    {herReply ? 'what you wrote back' : 'your half'}
                </p>

                {herReply && (
                    <div class="lt-reply-card">
                        <div class="lt-prose">
                            {replyParagraphs(herReply).map((para) => <p>{para}</p>)}
                        </div>
                        {herRepliedAt > 0 && (
                            /* The stored timestamp is epoch millis, so it is turned
                               into a WING date before it is labelled. Formatting it
                               in the reader's own timezone would mean a reply she
                               sent at 11pm reads as the next day on one device and
                               not on another. */
                            <p class="lt-dateline lt-dateline--after">
                                you sent this {wingDateLabel(wingDate(new Date(herRepliedAt)), { withYear: true })}
                            </p>
                        )}
                    </div>
                )}

                {/* <details> rather than two markup paths, and `open` only when
                    she has not answered yet: a letter she has already
                    answered should look answered, not like a big empty form.
                    It collapses natively with no JavaScript at all. */}
                <details class="lt-write" open={!herReply}>
                    <summary class="lt-write-summary">
                        {herReply ? 'write a different one' : 'write back'}
                    </summary>

                    <form
                        class="dj-form"
                        id="letter-form"
                        method="post"
                        action="/api/us/letter"
                    >
                        <input type="hidden" name="id" value={reading.id}>

                        <div class="dj-field">
                            <label class="dj-label" for="letter-reply">
                                Your letter back
                                <span class="dj-opt">&mdash; as long as you want, up to {REPLY_MAX}</span>
                            </label>
                            {/* maxlength matches REPLY_MAX in letters.ts, which the
                                endpoint also checks. The browser stopping her at the
                                limit is friendlier than the server refusing a
                                paragraph she has already written. */}
                            <textarea
                                class="dj-textarea"
                                id="letter-reply"
                                name="reply"
                                rows="12"
                                maxlength={REPLY_MAX}
                            >{herReply}</textarea>
                            {/* aria-hidden on purpose, and therefore deliberately NOT
                                wired up as aria-describedby: a counter that fires on
                                every keystroke is hostile to a screen reader, and
                                pointing a description at a hidden node produces an
                                empty description rather than a useful one. The label
                                above states the number once, where it belongs, and
                                maxlength enforces it without being announced. */}
                            <p class="lt-count" id="letter-count" aria-hidden="true">
                                {herReply.length} / {REPLY_MAX}
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <button class="dj-submit" id="letter-submit" type="submit">
                            {herReply ? 'replace it' : 'send it'}
                        </button>

                        {/* The enhancement script's channel. aria-live so the outcome
                            is announced without moving focus; hidden until there is
                            something to say. */}
                        <p class="notice" id="letter-status" role="status" aria-live="polite" hidden></p>
                    </form>
                </details>
            </section>

            <nav class="lt-column lt-nav" aria-label="other letters">
                {older ? (
                    <a href={`/stronger/vault/letters?read=${encodeURIComponent(older.id)}`}>
                        <span class="lt-nav-label" aria-hidden="true">&larr;</span>
                        the one before
                    </a>
                ) : (
                    <span>this is the first one</span>
                )}
                {newer ? (
                    <a href={`/stronger/vault/letters?read=${encodeURIComponent(newer.id)}`}>
                        the one after
                        <span class="lt-nav-label lt-nav-label--after" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
                    </a>
                ) : (
                    <span>that is the last one</span>
                )}
            </nav>

            <hr class="studio-rule">

            <div class="studio-foot">
                <span class="studio-tag">Stronger for it.</span>
                <a href="/stronger/vault/letters">&larr; all the letters</a>
            </div>
        </>
    ) : (
        /* ==============================================================
           VIEW ONE — THE SHELF
           ============================================================== */
        <>
            <p class="studio-kicker">line 03 &middot; letters</p>

            <h1 class="us-mark">
                <span class="us-mark-bracket" aria-hidden="true">[</span>letters<span
                      class="us-mark-bracket" aria-hidden="true">]</span>
            </h1>

            <p class="studio-lede">
                Everywhere else here makes me keep it short, {her}. Not this page.
            </p>

            {statusLine() && (
                <p class="lt-status-line" data-quiet={totals.unread > 0 ? '0' : '1'}>
                    {statusLine()}
                </p>
            )}

            {askedForMissing && (
                <p class="notice" data-kind="bad" role="status">
                    hm &mdash; I could not find that one. Nothing is lost; everything
                    there is is below.
                </p>
            )}

            {sealedRequest && (
                /* Reached when she opens a link to a letter that is still
                   sealed. Refused HERE, before any body could have been
                   rendered — and there is nothing to refuse anyway, because
                   visibleLetter() already removed it. */
                <p class="notice" data-kind="warn" role="status">
                    that one is still sealed
                    {sealedRequest.opensOn && <> until {wingDateLabel(sealedRequest.opensOn, { withYear: true })}</>}.
                    It opens on its own.
                </p>
            )}

            {errorText && (
                <p class="notice" data-kind="bad" role="status">hm &mdash; {errorText}</p>
            )}
            {justSent && !errorText && (
                <p class="notice" data-kind="ok" role="status">sent &mdash; I will read it.</p>
            )}

            {/* Dev only, both of them. She should never be shown a sentence about
                environment variables or serverless instances; on /stronger/dj the
                equivalent warnings are permanent, because there they are
                actionable. */}
            {import.meta.env.DEV && usingPlaceholders() && (
                <p class="notice" data-kind="warn">
                    dev &mdash; US_LETTERS is not set, so these are the committed
                    PLACEHOLDER letters. Nothing below is real.
                </p>
            )}
            {import.meta.env.DEV && lettersTier() === 'memory' && (
                <p class="notice" data-kind="warn">
                    dev &mdash; in-process store. Read receipts and replies are
                    non-durable and per-instance.
                </p>
            )}

            {nothingAtAll ? (
                <>
                    <hr class="studio-rule">
                    <p class="lt-empty">
                        Nothing here yet. Which means I have not written one yet, not
                        that there is nothing to write.
                    </p>
                </>
            ) : (
                <>
                    {board.waiting.length > 0 && (
                        <>
                            <hr class="studio-rule">
                            <h2 class="lt-section-head">waiting for you</h2>
                            {/* A <ul>, because this is a list of things and a screen
                                reader should be told how many. Oldest first: a letter
                                is part of a sequence, and number five arrives before
                                the thing number three explains otherwise. See
                                shelf() in letters.ts. */}
                            <ul class="lt-shelf">
                                {board.waiting.map((letter, i) => (
                                    <li>
                                        <a
                                            class={i === 0 ? 'lt-card lt-card--hero' : 'lt-card'}
                                            href={`/stronger/vault/letters?read=${encodeURIComponent(letter.id)}`}
                                        >
                                            <span class="lt-new">new</span>
                                            <h3 class={letter.title ? 'lt-card-title' : 'lt-card-title lt-card-title--untitled'}>
                                                {titleOf(letter)}
                                            </h3>
                                            {dayLabel(letter.written) && (
                                                <p class="lt-card-meta lt-card-meta--under">
                                                    written {dayLabel(letter.written)}
                                                </p>
                                            )}
                                        </a>
                                    </li>
                                ))}
                            </ul>
                        </>
                    )}

                    {board.again.length > 0 && (
                        <>
                            <hr class="studio-rule">
                            <h2 class="lt-section-head">read again</h2>
                            <ul class="lt-shelf">
                                {board.again.map((letter) => (
                                    <li>
                                        <a
                                            class="lt-card"
                                            href={`/stronger/vault/letters?read=${encodeURIComponent(letter.id)}`}
                                        >
                                            <p class="lt-card-meta">
                                                {[
                                                    dayLabel(letter.written),
                                                    (states[letter.id]?.reply ?? '') ? 'you answered' : '',
                                                ].filter(Boolean).join(' · ')}
                                            </p>
                                            <h3 class={letter.title ? 'lt-card-title' : 'lt-card-title lt-card-title--untitled'}>
                                                {titleOf(letter)}
                                            </h3>
                                            {/* The first line, and only on a letter she
                                                has already read. On an unread card this
                                                would be a spoiler; here it is the thing
                                                that reminds her which letter this is. */}
                                            {letter.paragraphs[0] && (
                                                <p class="lt-card-excerpt">{letter.paragraphs[0]}</p>
                                            )}
                                        </a>
                                    </li>
                                ))}
                            </ul>
                        </>
                    )}

                    {board.sealed.length > 0 && (
                        <>
                            <hr class="studio-rule">
                            <h2 class="lt-section-head">not yet</h2>
                            <ul class="lt-shelf">
                                {board.sealed.map((letter) => (
                                    /* A <li> holding a <div>, never an <a
                                       aria-disabled>: a disabled link is still a link
                                       to a keyboard and to most screen readers, and
                                       aria-disabled does not prevent activation. No
                                       href, no problem — us.css's .room makes the same
                                       call.

                                       There is no title and no body in this object.
                                       Not hidden: absent. */
                                    <li>
                                        <div class="lt-card lt-card--sealed">
                                            <span class="lt-seal">sealed</span>
                                            <p class="lt-opens">
                                                {letter.opensOn
                                                    ? `opens ${wingDateLabel(letter.opensOn, { withYear: true })}`
                                                    : 'opens when it opens'}
                                            </p>
                                            {letter.teaser && <p class="lt-teaser">{letter.teaser}</p>}
                                        </div>
                                    </li>
                                ))}
                            </ul>
                        </>
                    )}
                </>
            )}

            <hr class="studio-rule">

            <div class="studio-foot">
                <span class="studio-tag">Stronger for it.</span>
                <a href="/stronger/vault">&larr; back to the room</a>
            </div>
        </>
    )}
</main>

<script is:inline define:vars={{ replyMax: REPLY_MAX }}>
/* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   TWO JOBS, AND DELIBERATELY NO MORE.

   Rendered unconditionally rather than wrapped in a template expression: a
   <script> inside `{...}` asks the Astro compiler to read JavaScript braces as
   template braces, and the failure mode is a build error in a file that reads as
   correct. Each block below bails out on its own if its markup is absent — which
   it is on the shelf view.

   1. HER LETTER BACK, WITHOUT LOSING WHAT SHE TYPED. This is the only one that
      matters. The form already works without any of this: 303, re-render, done.
      What a native submit cannot do is fail without navigating away, and on this
      page the thing it would take with it is several hundred words. Everything
      this block buys is: on failure, her letter is still in the textarea.

   2. THE CHARACTER COUNT. Visual only, aria-hidden, because a live region that
      fires on every keystroke is hostile to a screen reader and `maxlength`
      already enforces the limit without being announced.

   WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT: a localStorage draft. It is the obvious next idea
   for a long textarea and it was rejected on purpose. It would persist her private
   words outside the session cookie's lifetime, on disk, surviving sign-out — a new
   privacy surface in the one wing built around not having any, in exchange for
   covering a crash that job 1 already covers for the failure that actually
   happens. If it is ever added, it must be cleared on sign-out and on a successful
   send, and that is two more things to get right.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
(function () {
    var form = document.getElementById('letter-form');
    if (!form) return;  // the shelf view. Nothing to enhance.

    var submit = document.getElementById('letter-submit');
    var out = document.getElementById('letter-status');
    var box = document.getElementById('letter-reply');
    var counter = document.getElementById('letter-count');

    /* ---- 2. the counter ------------------------------------------------ */
    if (box && counter) {
        var paint = function () {
            var n = box.value.length;
            counter.textContent = n + ' / ' + replyMax;
            // The threshold is a warning, not a limit — maxlength is the limit.
            counter.dataset.over = n > replyMax - 100 ? '1' : '0';
        };
        box.addEventListener('input', paint);
        paint();
    }

    /* ---- 1. the send --------------------------------------------------- */
    if (!submit || !out || !box) return;

    var MESSAGES = {
        'no-such-letter': "I cannot find that letter any more. Nothing was sent.",
        sealed: 'that letter is sealed again somehow. Nothing was sent.',
        empty: 'there is nothing in the box yet.',
        'too-long': 'that is longer than the box will hold. Trim it a little and send again.',
        'bad-request': "the server couldn't read that. Nothing was sent.",
        unauthorized: 'your session ran out. Answer the questions again and you are back in.',
        unconfigured: 'something is unplugged on his end. Not your fault.',
        store: "that didn't save. Nothing was sent — your words are still here. Try again?",
        rate: 'give it a minute, then send it again.'
    };

    function say(kind, text) {
        // The word is part of the message, so this never depends on colour.
        out.dataset.kind = kind;
        out.textContent = (kind === 'bad' ? 'hm — ' : 'sent — ') + text;
        out.hidden = false;
    }

    var label = submit.textContent;

    form.addEventListener('submit', async function (event) {
        event.preventDefault();

        var body = {
            id: form.elements.id.value,
            reply: box.value
        };
        if (!body.reply.trim()) {
            say('bad', 'write something first.');
            box.focus();
            return;
        }

        submit.disabled = true;
        submit.textContent = 'sending';

        var res, data;
        try {
            res = await fetch('/api/us/letter', {
                // JSON in, JSON out. The endpoint keys its response shape off this
                // header, so the no-JS form POST still gets its redirect.
                method: 'POST',
                headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
                credentials: 'same-origin',
                body: JSON.stringify(body)
            });
            data = await res.json();
        } catch (err) {
            say('bad', 'the server did not answer. Nothing was sent, and what you wrote is still here.');
            return;
        } finally {
            submit.disabled = false;
            submit.textContent = label;
        }

        if (!res.ok || !data || data.ok !== true) {
            var code = (data && data.error) || 'no';
            say('bad', MESSAGES[code] || 'that did not go through. Nothing was sent.');
            return;
        }

        say('ok', 'it is on the shelf next to the letter. I will read it.');

        /* Hand control back to the SERVER rather than painting her card from this
           response. Patching it here would mean a second renderer in JavaScript —
           paragraph splitting, the date line, the open/closed state of the
           <details> — that can disagree with the Astro one, and a card that
           disagrees with the store is worse than a reload. The short pause is so
           the line above is readable first. */
        setTimeout(function () {
            window.location.assign(
                '/stronger/vault/letters?read=' + encodeURIComponent(data.id) + '&sent=1'
            );
        }, 700);
    });
})();
</script>
</body>
</html>
